[{"id":"DOM-1","kind":"missing","severity":"blocking","requirement":"none","rule":"D-12","title":"The data model records no currency","why":"The data model sketch stores `{ version: 1, nextOrderNumber: integer, customers: Customer[], orders: Order[] }` with no currency field, and A-03 only says \"All amounts are EUR\". D-12 says: \"A single-currency app still records the currency (ISO 4217 code) on the document or at the data root, so that a second currency later cannot be mistaken for the first.\" Right now the currency exists only as an assumption and in the display format of R-16.","suggested":"Data model sketch: `{ version: 1, currency: \"EUR\", nextOrderNumber: integer, customers: Customer[], orders: Order[] }`\n\n### R-17 [MUST] Persist across reloads\n- Every change is written to `localStorage` at once; after a reload all customers, orders and lines are as they were.\n- The stored data names its currency as an ISO 4217 code at the root (`currency: \"EUR\"`); stored data with no currency or another currency is treated as unreadable (R-18).","proven":true},
{"id":"DOM-2","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-12","rule":"D-15","title":"Nothing says the unit price excludes VAT","why":"R-12 says \"Unit price is entered in euros with at most two decimals ... and stored as whole cents\" but does not say whether VAT is in it. D-15 says \"lines keep unit price exclusive of VAT (a rate and a VAT amount can be added per line)\", and the Terms table defines Unit price as \"Price of one unit, exclusive of VAT\". If users type gross prices now, R-23 cannot add VAT later without guessing what each stored price means.","suggested":"### R-12 [MUST] Validate order lines\n- Unit price is the price of one unit excluding VAT, entered in euros with at most two decimals, from 0.00 to 1,000,000.00, and stored as whole cents; `12.5` becomes 1250 cents, `12.345` is refused. The line form labels the field \"Unit price (excl. VAT)\".","proven":true},
{"id":"DOM-3","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-24","rule":"D-06","title":"R-24 'Export or print an invoice' when the app has no invoices","why":"R-24 reads \"Export or print an invoice\", but the app has no invoice entity, only orders. D-06 says \"An order is not an invoice\" and \"The app must never present an order as an invoice.\" As written, R-24 can be read as printing an order with the title invoice. No current requirement says the UI never calls an order an invoice. Not proven: what R-24 should produce is not stated. It is settled once the owner says whether R-24 means issuing a real invoice from an order or printing the order.","suggested":"### R-24 [LATER] Issue an invoice from an order\n- An invoice is a separate document created from an order and refers to it; printing or exporting an order prints an order, titled \"Order\", never \"Invoice\".\n\n### R-20a [MUST] Orders are not invoices\n- No screen, label or export calls an order an invoice.","proven":false},
{"id":"DOM-4","kind":"risk","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-24","rule":"D-09","title":"The data model cannot hold the content Article 226 requires on an invoice","why":"D-09 says: \"Anything the app calls an invoice must be able to show all of these\": issue date, sequential number, supplier VAT number, customer VAT number where relevant, full name and address of supplier and customer, VAT rate and amount. The data model has `Customer { id, name, email, phone }`, A-02 says \"No address (confirmed)\", and there is no supplier record at all. R-24 has no place in the data model for these fields.","suggested":"### R-24 [LATER] Issue an invoice from an order\n- Needs, before it can be built: a supplier record (legal name, address, VAT number), customer address and VAT number (added fields, existing ones kept), an issue date, a supply date when it differs, an invoice number from a series, and the VAT rate and amount per rate (R-23).","proven":true},
{"id":"DOM-5","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-24","rule":"D-07","title":"Nothing says an issued invoice is immutable","why":"D-07 says: \"An issued invoice is immutable. Corrections are credit notes or new documents that refer to it.\" R-24 carries no such constraint, and the order flows it would build on are freely editable and deletable (R-08 \"The order date can be changed\", R-09 \"removes the order together with its lines\", R-13, R-14).","suggested":"### R-24 [LATER] Issue an invoice from an order\n- An issued invoice cannot be edited or deleted; a correction is a credit note that refers to it.\n- An order that has an invoice can no longer be edited or deleted.","proven":true},
{"id":"DOM-6","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-24","rule":"D-08","title":"Invoice numbering is not stated and must not reuse the order counter","why":"D-08 says invoice numbers are \"sequential, drawn from one or more series, and unique\", that \"The number is assigned at issue, not at draft\", and that \"Order numbers in this app (A-04) are a separate counter and are not invoice numbers.\" The data model has one counter only, `nextOrderNumber`. R-24 does not say where an invoice number comes from.","suggested":"### R-24 [LATER] Issue an invoice from an order\n- An invoice gets its number at issue, from its own sequential series stored apart from `nextOrderNumber`; numbers are unique and never reused.","proven":true},
{"id":"DOM-7","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-22","rule":"D-06","title":"'paid' is used as an order status","why":"R-22 reads \"Order status (draft, sent, paid)\". The domain skill says \"A payment settles an invoice in full or in part\", and D-06 says \"No tax obligation and no claim exist until an invoice is issued.\" An order creates no claim, so it cannot be paid. 'paid' belongs to an invoice.","suggested":"### R-22 [LATER] Order status (draft, confirmed, fulfilled); payment status (unpaid, partly paid, paid) belongs to the invoice (R-24)","proven":true},
{"id":"DOM-8","kind":"unclear","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-07","rule":"D-13","title":"The order date is stored under the generic name `date`","why":"The data model stores `Order { ..., date: \"YYYY-MM-DD\", ... }`. D-13 says \"The order date in this app is none of these; it must not be reused as an issue date.\" D-10 says fields should be added later \"without migrating or renaming the existing ones\". A field called `date` invites reuse as the issue date or supply date once invoices exist. Not proven: the rule does not name the field. It is settled by the owner choosing the field name before the model is final.","suggested":"Data model sketch: `Order { id: string (uuid), number: integer, customerId: Customer.id, orderDate: \"YYYY-MM-DD\", lines: OrderLine[] }`","proven":false},
{"id":"DOM-9","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-06","rule":"D-14","title":"No criterion says search, filter and sort never write","why":"R-06 trims the search text (\"ignoring leading and trailing spaces\") and R-10 sorts (\"newest date first\"), but no criterion says they leave stored data unchanged. D-14 says: \"Search, filter and sort never change data\" and \"A filter that writes (for example re-sorting and saving lines, or trimming a name while matching) corrupts the stored state silently.\"","suggested":"### R-06 [MUST] Search customers\n- Searching, filtering (R-10) and sorting (A-07) never change or save stored data; after clearing the search, the stored customers and orders are unchanged.","proven":true},
{"id":"DOM-10","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-01","rule":"D-10","title":"The customer name is not stated to be the legal name","why":"R-01 says only \"A customer with a non-empty name (after trimming) is saved\". D-10 says \"the name must be the legal name, not a nickname\", because Article 226(5) requires the customer's full name on an invoice.","suggested":"### R-01 [MUST] Create a customer\n- A customer with a non-empty name (after trimming) is saved and appears in the customer list; the field is labelled \"Legal name\" and holds the customer's full legal name.","proven":true},
{"id":"DOM-11","kind":"missing","severity":"advisory","requirement":"R-05","rule":"D-11","title":"Deleting a customer is not ready for documents that must be kept","why":"R-05 says the app tells the user that the orders \"must be deleted first\", and then the customer can be removed. D-11 says \"the same rule, without exception, must hold for invoices. A customer may be deactivated, never removed.\" No requirement covers deactivating a customer, and once R-24 exists, the 'delete the orders first' path would orphan invoices that must be kept.","suggested":"### R-05 [MUST] Delete a customer\n- A customer that still has orders cannot be deleted; the app says how many orders they have and that those must be deleted first. Nothing is removed.\n- Once invoices exist (R-24), a customer with any invoice can never be deleted, only deactivated.","proven":true}]
